      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's start by substituting the value of $b+d$ into the given equation. We have:\n\n\\[\nab + bc + cd + da = 30\n\\]\n\nSubstitute $b+d = 5$ into the equation:\n\n\\[\nab + bc + cd + da = 30\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out $b$ and $d$ from the terms involving $b$ and $d$:\n\n\\[\nab + bc + cd + da = b(a+c) + d(a+c)\n\\]\n\nNow, we can factor out $(a+c)$ from the right side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nab + bc + cd + da = (a+c)(b+d)\n\\]\n\nWe know that $b+d = 5$, so we can substitute that value into the equation:\n\n\\[\nab + bc + cd + da = (a+c) \\cdot 5\n\\]\n\nNow, we can divide both sides of the equation by 5:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{ab + bc + cd + da}{5} = a + c\n\\]\n\nSince we know that $ab + bc + cd + da = 30$, we can substitute that value into the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{30}{5} = a + c\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n6 = a + c\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
